t big data we have  this thing called soup  because cells can  can break up and we can get a bit of rna  from other cells  this is one type of background and  single cell and asic data and then in  wta data  um we have the background binding which  we need a slightly different  model for than the single cell rnase  data and then  the sensitivity was actually quite  comparable so that was great so  we find a similar number of genes  detected so often  most compared to most single silent  datasets  fantastic and  let me give a few more seconds there  since we didn't get a chance to talk to  you alexander earlier about your model  um when we cut off i'm gonna  phrase a question towards you and then  maybe you can jump back in on that  um so when you were working on building  your model  for analyzing the geomics wta data  what were some of the considerations and  background that you took from  other approaches that informed the model  that you developed  that you were working with with the wta  data  um yeah there was already quite a bit  available  people have thought for a while um  about um things like sampling noise that  we get from technology such as rna  sequencing also because  we never actually catch all the rna  whatever essay that we're using  and this process itself creates a little  bit of noise because we only capture  maybe one percent  five percent of the rna in the sale and  people have also thought about  generally how can we summarize the  expression of a cell biologically  using uh deep neural networks or using  something simple such as principle  component analysis factor analysis  so we could reuse these parts for  wta then  wta has negative probes available  this gave us some information about the  background binding which we need to  account for in  wta and then um wta  um yeah it doesn't have quite as many  um quite as many samples usually i mean  the typical experiments  as a single cell rna sequencing because  in single cell rna sequencing we can  sometimes have  millions of cells even sometimes a few  thousands  um so in a typical wta experiment maybe  we have information from 50 or 100  samples maybe soon more  and then you sort of need to adjust the  models a little bit so you don't  over fit the data and you need to make  slightly less  less complex models  great thanks for that perspective now  that's really uh really important to  keep in mind uh as this data has so much  uh ability to fit the type of model and  type of experiment that you want to do  um it has that flexibility in the roi  selection  that does bring up another point that i  wanted to pull the room a little bit  about  which was the role in which developing  uh broader access data sets might help  inform the community  and what role that there's that maybe  some of you  work on in terms of cross-institutional  consortia  might play in developing new resources  for the community  i know for example christina you're part  of h10  and i was interested in your perspective  on how the community might collaborate  in the future on these types of projects  sure so within h10 uh there's  which is the human tumor atlas network  there's been um  fairly extensive efforts to try and uh  generalize generate  orthogonal spatial and single cell  single cell resolution data sets on the  same material  as a means to inform the development of  analytic tools and  in some ways that's that's still  plugging along um  and i think that as we've heard from  from many of the speakers right  a big push going forward is really going  to be on the analytics and how we  interpret and leverage uh the most  information from these  um rich biological data sets i certainly  think that there's value  in establishing standards and best  practices both for  approach but also for the downstream  analytics and that of course takes time  you know we need to spend time with the  data to understand what's optimal and  this is still a very  um nascent field uh i also believe  though that  as you point out right having um  sufficiently representative cohorts that  have been profiled right  that allow us to understand the  composition topography of different cell  populations could be really valuable  um clearly there's going to be context  dependencies and i think in cancer  that's something we wrestle with  a lot there's the the tissue  dependencies there's the temporal  and the state of disease but i do think  having reference  data sets could help on many fronts um  in terms of moving the field forward  chris or omar anything you'd like to add  to that  all right i think some of the uh i guess  that's some of the things i'm also  looking for some of the  better measures of heterogeneity that  we're seeing themselves can be a metric  we've seen some of this from the bulk  field is that  heterogeneity itself epigenetically  especially can  indicate uh have a prognostic value in  indicated risk and so i think we'll  start to see this probably  also come out of the spatial data as  more jacksonville  great thank you all so much  i think this is we're getting pretty  close to time here for you so  i think we're going to wrap up our panel  discussion uh i want to thank  all of our speakers for the fantastic  conversation that we've had today  uh and i hope that this has been a  thought-provoking exploration  of the analytical needs and the future  for spatial  genomics to integrate with the  analytical toolkits  that people have available for their  next waves of scientific exploration  in addition to the great work described  by the panelists here today  i'd like to point out that nanastring is  excited to announce the launch of  geomet's translational leadership  network the gtln  which is aimed at again helping develop  some of these shared resources  tools and facilitating conversations  across  large institutions and has been  working with breast cancer researchers  in one case as well as covid  researchers in another case we are n